  "id": "gcp-hybrid-dr-recovery-site-v1",
  "title": "Cloud recovery site for an on-premises application",
  "subtitle": "An on-premises application backs up data and maintains a cloud recovery copy that can be activated after a disruption. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nA business-critical on-premises application needs a recovery location without moving its primary operation immediately to a cloud-only model.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nThe on-premises workload periodically backs up or replicates selected data over a secured connection to Azure. Azure Backup, Azure Site Recovery, or application-native replication may protect different components. A warm or cold Azure application environment is prepared according to recovery objectives; an operator activates the recovery workflow and updates traffic routing after validating dependencies and data state.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThe Google source explicitly says recovery frequency and active/passive state depend on RTO and RPO, and notes that hybrid systems require reliable secure networking and added operational effort. Azure Site Recovery is not a universal substitute for database-native consistency or application-specific recovery. The catalog should show activation as an operator-controlled step and should not imply failover is automatic or that a tested RTO is known.\n\nSource: [Google Cloud Architecture Center](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/hybrid).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: Google Cloud\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [Google Cloud hybrid deployment archetype](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/hybrid)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- Product and recovery workflow must be selected by workload; no RTO/RPO, replication mode, or automatic failover is asserted.",
